### Express example showing wildcard route with req.params array

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/master/examples/downloads/index.js

This example demonstrates a wildcard route `/files/*file`. The comment states that without naming, `/files/*` would be accessed via `req.params[0]`, but by naming the wildcard `*file`, the captured path segments become available as an array on `req.params.file`. The `.join('/')` call reconstructs the sub-path from the array.

```javascript
// /files/* is accessed via req.params[0]
// but here we name it :file
app.get('/files/*file', function (req, res, next) {
  res.download(req.params.file.join('/'), { root: FILES_DIR }, function (err) {
    if (!err) return; // file sent
    if (err.status !== 404) return next(err); // non-404 error
    // file for download not found
    res.statusCode = 404;
    res.send('Cant find that file, sorry!');
  });
});
```

--------------------------------

### Wildcard Named Splat and Optional Group

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/master/test/app.router.js

Test block demonstrating path-to-regexp v8 wildcard syntax (`*user`) that captures multiple segments as an array, and optional brace syntax (`{*user}`) for zero-or-more segment matching. The `*user` pattern matches one or many segments (returning an array via `req.params.user`), while `{/*user}` wraps the wildcard in braces to make it optional (matching zero segments).

```javascript
  describe(':name*', function () {
    it('should match one segment', function (done) {
      var app = express()

      app.get('/user/*user', function (req, res) {
        res.end(req.params.user[0])
      })

      request(app)
        .get('/user/122')
        .expect('122', done)
    })

    it('should match many segments', function (done) {
      var app = express()

      app.get('/user/*user', function (req, res) {
        res.end(req.params.user.join('/'))
      })

      request(app)
        .get('/user/1/2/3/4')
        .expect('1/2/3/4', done)
    })

    it('should match zero segments', function (done) {
      var app = express()

      app.get('/user{/*user}', function (req, res) {
        res.end(req.params.user)
      })

      request(app)
        .get('/user')
        .expect('', done)
    })
  })
```

--------------------------------

### Changelog: router@2.0.0-beta.1 breaking changes for wildcards

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/master/History.md

The router@2.0.0-beta.1 upgrade with path-to-regexp@3.2.0 removed the special `*` path segment behavior (so bare `app.all('*')` no longer works) and added new `?`, `*`, and `+` parameter modifiers — enabling the `/*splat` named wildcard syntax.

```markdown
  * deps: router@2.0.0-beta.1
    - Add new `?`, `*`, and `+` parameter modifiers
    - Internalize private `router.process_params` method
    - Matching group expressions are only RegExp syntax
    - Named matching groups no longer available by position in `req.params`
    - Regular expressions can only be used in a matching group
    - Remove `debug` dependency
    - Special `*` path segment behavior removed
    - deps: array-flatten@3.0.0
    - deps: parseurl@~1.3.3
    - deps: path-to-regexp@3.2.0
    - deps: setprototypeof@1.2.0
```

--------------------------------

### Wildcard route `/*splat` named parameter replaces `*`

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/master/test/app.all.js

The `/*splat` pattern with path-to-regexp v3 (v8) named wildcard parameter replaces the old `*` wildcard. The matched value is available as `req.params.splat`. The old special `*` path segment behavior was removed in router@2.0.0-beta.1 with the upgrade to path-to-regexp@3.2.0.

```javascript
app.all('/*splat', function(req, res, next){
  if (n++) return done(new Error('DELETE called several times'));
  next();
});
```

